I think this can fail. Suppose first `_Lock` was called, i.e., the write lock was taken, and then `_RLock`. As is, this means one ends up with `level=1`. Now suppose the code was buggy and `_Unlock` was called before  `_RUnLock`. Then, `_UnLock` would have found `level=1`, decreased it to 0, and here one would just pass through. In principle, this would fail the `assert` just below, but in non-debug mode one would release the write lock without setting thread id to 0. 

I think this can be solved by changing the logic slightly: when one takes the write lock the first time, already set `level = 1` (and inside `_UnLock`, unconditionally decrease the level and return if still non-zero).
That way, here one can simply call `_UnLock` -- it would generally just decrease the level and in the buggy order example, it would also release the lock, setting writer id to zero. 

Possibly that also allows to simplify `_RLock` as in principle one can just check whether `level > 0` to know whether a write lock is in place, i.e., one could do something like
