I don't know if this is worth doing, but in looking at this (and some of the performance work around EVP objects), I noted that alot of our algorithms have several fields in common.  Do you think it might be worth re-organizing them so that there was a common embedded struct at the start of each type specific struct? i.e.

```
struct evp_obj {
     int type;
     int origin;
     CRYPTO_REF_COUNT refcnt;
     ...
};

struct evp_md_st {
     struct evp_obj base; /* must be first */
     ... additional type specific data...
};

struct evp_cipher_st {
     struct evp_obj base; /*must be first */
    ... additional type specific data...
};
```

That way all EVP_* objects could be cast to a struct evp_obj and the respective receiver functions could either just treat them as the evp_obj they are, or cast them to their type specific variant (if so needed).  That would let you avoid having to create thunking functions.  Not sure if thats worth while though.

